-- title:
--   Quotient of a p-divisible group by a closed subgroup
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local domain which is a principal ideal ring, let $K$ be a field that is an $R$-algebra and a fraction field of $R$ with $\operatorname{char} K = 0$, and let $p$ be prime. Let $G$ and $T$ be $p$-divisible groups over $R$ of heights $h$ and $t$: families of commutative cocommutative Hopf $R$-algebras $G.\mathrm{level}\,v$, finite and free over $R$ of rank $p^{v\cdot h}$ (resp. $p^{v\cdot t}$), with surjective bialgebra transition maps $\mathrm{level}(v+1)\to\mathrm{level}\,v$ whose kernel is the ideal generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^{v}$. Let $\pi_v : G.\mathrm{level}\,v \to T.\mathrm{level}\,v$ be surjective $R$-bialgebra maps with $\pi_{v+1}$ followed by $T.\mathrm{transition}\,v$ equal to $G.\mathrm{transition}\,v$ followed by $\pi_v$. The assertion is that there exist $h_B\in\mathbb N$, a $p$-divisible group $B$ over $R$ of height $h_B$, and bialgebra maps $j_v : B.\mathrm{level}\,v \to G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ such that $h = t + h_B$; each $j_v$ is injective; the range of $j_v$ as an $R$-algebra map is the Hopf kernel of $\pi_v$, namely the subalgebra of $G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ on which $(\mathrm{id}\otimes\pi_v)\circ\Delta$ agrees with $a\mapsto a\otimes 1$; $j_{v+1}$ followed by $G.\mathrm{transition}\,v$ equals $B.\mathrm{transition}\,v$ followed by $j_v$; and, for every algebraically closed field $L$ that is an $R$-algebra and every $v$, in the convolution groups of $R$-algebra maps to $L$: an $x\in G.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$ restricts along $j_v$ to the identity of $B.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$ exactly when $x$ is the composite of $\pi_v$ with some $y\in T.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$, and every $b\in B.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$ is the restriction along $j_v$ of some $x\in G.\mathrm{Point}\,L\,v$.
--
--   This is the existence of the quotient of a $p$-divisible group $G$ by a closed $p$-divisible subgroup $T$, over a local principal ideal domain with fraction field of characteristic zero: the Hopf kernels of the $\pi_v$ form a $p$-divisible group $B$ of height $h-t$, and on points over an algebraically closed field the sequence $1\to T_v(L)\to G_v(L)\to B_v(L)\to 1$ is exact. It is used in the analysis of the Néron model of a modular curve at $p$, where the toric part is split off as such a quotient.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_PDivisibleGroup_exists_pDivisibleGroup_bialgHom_injective_range_eq_hopfKer_of_surjective_of_comp_eq_of_isPrincipalIdealRing.lean

